<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/testflight-128x128_2x_27-1682469284350.png" alt=""><p class="image-caption"></p></div>
<p id="p2">I test a lot of apps, so I have strong opinions about <a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/testflight/id899247664" rel="noopener noreferrer">TestFlight</a>. However I usually keep those opinions to myself because despite the fact that it’s critical to my work, TestFlight wasn’t built for app reviewers. Most people haven’t accumulated hundreds of active and inactive betas in the app like I have, and for them, TestFlight is probably fine. However, for me, TestFlight is <a href="https://www.macstories.net/stories/testfights-inability-to-handle-large-beta-collections-needs-to-be-fixed/" rel="noopener noreferrer">a constant source</a> <a href="https://www.macstories.net/news/a-testflight-update-patched-but-still-broken/" rel="noopener noreferrer">of frustration</a>.</p>
<p id="p3">So, you can imagine my excitement when I read <a href="https://9to5mac.com/2026/07/21/apple-just-improved-testflight-for-users-with-a-lot-of-beta-apps/" rel="noopener noreferrer">a post by Zac Hall at 9to5Mac</a> that a search field had been added to TestFlight. “Finally,” I thought to myself. I stopped what I was doing and updated the app immediately. After all, <a href="https://www.macstories.net/news/a-testflight-update-patched-but-still-broken/" rel="noopener noreferrer">search was my number one request</a> for TestFlight back in 2024.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/07/21/cleanshot-2026-07-21-at-1640372x-1784666477341-2316ae957a.png" alt="An apostrophe gets you the top spot in Apple's new alphabetical TestFlight list."><p class="image-caption">An apostrophe gets you the top spot in Apple’s new alphabetical TestFlight list.</p></div>
<p id="p5">That 2024 wishlist was published in the wake of a bad stretch the app had <a href="https://www.macstories.net/stories/testfights-inability-to-handle-large-beta-collections-needs-to-be-fixed/" rel="noopener noreferrer">beginning in 2023</a>. At the time, TestFlight was plagued by laggy performance if you were on a lot of betas. To Apple’s credit, they fixed the performance issues, though the issues have begun to creep back into recent releases. Still, the past couple of years as a TestFlight user have been fine despite all the ways <a href="https://www.macstories.net/news/a-testflight-update-patched-but-still-broken/" rel="noopener noreferrer">the app could be made better</a> for users.</p>
<p id="p6">When the TestFlight update finished installing, I eagerly opened it on my Mac and then my iPhone. Sure enough, there was the search field at long last. Lists practically beg for a search field, and finally someone at Apple added one, and to whoever built that feature, a huge thanks from me and the MacStories team.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/07/21/merged-framed-small-1784666131558-19fe7ff6d5.mp4" alt=""><p class="image-caption"></p></div>
<p id="p8">Unfortunately, this story comes with one very big “but.” Previously, TestFlight betas were listed in reverse chronological order, which I liked. The newer beta releases were at the top of the list and the older ones that were on the verge of being replaced by an App Store release were at the bottom, along with expired betas, a list that I never really understood the purpose of.</p>
<p id="p9">Well, those expired betas are now scattered among the active TestFlight betas in an alphabetical list and it seems that the expired beta list has been expanded to include every single beta I’ve ever been a part of. I did a rough count and for me that’s nearly 800 app listings. As you can imagine, the active betas in that list are few and far between. Search helps, but scrolling is no longer an option.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/07/21/shortcut-upload-1784666546339-446b2d58d5.png" alt="The live betas are few and far between when mixed with betas gone by."><p class="image-caption">The live betas are few and far between when mixed with betas gone by.</p></div>
<p id="p11">I get that this is a very “me and a few of my friends” problem, and search does help, but it’s not enough. The active to inactive beta ratio is so bad that few iPhone screens include more than two or three active betas. So here’s my proposal:</p>
<ul id="ul12"><li>Add a toggle in TestFlight’s settings that hides inactive betas.</li>
</ul><p id="p13">That’s it, that would make all the difference in the world. I’d love to have the reverse chronological ordering back, a performance tune-up, and the other things I wished for back in 2024, but I’ll live with hiding all the noise from my beta list.</p>
